tel 1.0

base clavichord
import soundness

info   a keypress as a value: a character, a named key, or either held with modifiers
detail
    _Clavichord_ is the vocabulary of keys shared by everything in Soundness that reads or
    produces a keypress. Terminals and browsers agree on what a keypress *is* and disagree
    only on how it is encoded, so the model lives here, apart from either: profanity decodes
    a terminal's escape sequences into `Keypress` values, and tarantula renders them as the
    key actions the WebDriver protocol defines, without either knowing about the other.

    A `Keypress` is an ordinary character, a function key, one of the named editing keys, an
    otherwise unnamed escape sequence, or one of those wrapped in the modifiers held with it.
    A modifier accepts only what can meaningfully be modified, so the type rules out
    keypresses no keyboard can produce.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    def describe(keypress: Keypress): Text = keypress match
      case Keypress.CharKey(char)              => t"typed $char"
      case Keypress.Ctrl('C')                  => t"interrupt"
      case Keypress.Shift(Keypress.Enter)      => t"new line"
      case Keypress.Up | Keypress.Down         => t"history"
      case other                               => other.show

    Keypress.Ctrl(Keypress.Alt(Keypress.Left)).show   // t"[⌃]+[⌥]+[←]"
    ```

    The library has a single source set, `clavichord.core`, for every platform, and
    everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella.

refer clavichord.Keypress

entry .Keypress   a key as pressed: a character, a named or function key, or one held with modifiers
  detail
      An enum whose cases are:

      - the named editing keys, `Tab`, `Enter`, `Backspace`, `Delete`, `Escape`, `Insert`,
        `Home`, `End`, `PageUp`, `PageDown`, `Left`, `Right`, `Up` and `Down`, each also a
        `Keypress.Editing`;
      - `CharKey(char)`, an ordinary character, with any shift already applied (a shifted
        `a` is `CharKey('A')`);
      - `FunctionKey(number)`, `F1` to `F12` and beyond;
      - `EscapeSeq(id, content*)`, an escape sequence with no other name, by its final
        identifying character and its parameters;
      - and the modifiers, which nest outwards in a fixed order: `Shift` of an editing or
        function key; `Alt` of those or a `Shift`; `Ctrl` of those, an `Alt`, or one of the
        characters a control code can carry (`'A'` to `'Z'`, `'['`, `'\\'`, `']'`, `'^'`,
        `'_'` and `'@'`); and `Meta` of any of the other modifiers or keys.

      The modifiers' parameter types follow that order, so `Shift(CharKey('a'))` and
      `Alt(Ctrl('C'))` do not compile: `Ctrl(Alt(Left))` is the one way to write that
      combination, and a match need consider only one.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Keypress.Ctrl('C')
      Keypress.Shift(Keypress.Enter)
      Keypress.Meta(Keypress.Ctrl(Keypress.Alt(Keypress.Shift(Keypress.Home))))
      Keypress.Shift(Keypress.CharKey('a'))   // does not compile
      ```

      A keypress shows (`.showable`) with a Unicode symbol in square brackets for each key
      and modifier, joined to what it modifies with `+`.
  refer clavichord.Keypress.EditKey
  entry .Editing   the marker trait of the fourteen named editing keys
      `Tab`, `Home`, `End`, `PageUp`, `PageDown`, `Insert`, `Delete`, `Enter`, `Backspace`,
      `Escape`, `Left`, `Right`, `Up` and `Down` each extend it. It is a nominal marker
      rather than a union of the cases' singleton types so that it still admits them when
      they are reached through the umbrella's export forwarders, as `soundness.Keypress.Enter`.
  entry .EditKey   any one of the named editing keys: `Keypress & Keypress.Editing`
      The type of a key which `Shift`, `Alt`, `Ctrl` and `Meta` may wrap besides function
      keys, and the type to use for a method which handles only editing keys.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val key: Keypress.EditKey = Keypress.PageDown
      ```
  entry .showable   shows a keypress with a bracketed Unicode symbol per key and modifier
      Shift is `[⇧]`, Alt `[⌥]`, Ctrl `[⌃]` and Meta `[⌘]`, each joined to what it
      modifies with `+`. The editing keys are `[⇥]`, `[↵]`, `[⌫]`, `[⌦]`, `[⎋]`, `[⎀]`, the
      arrows `[↑]` `[↓]` `[←]` `[→]`, `[↖]` for Home, `[↘]` for End, and `[⇞]` `[⇟]` for the
      page keys. A character is bracketed as itself, except a space, `[␣]`; a function key is
      `[F5]`; and an escape sequence is `[⎋R]`, showing its identifier but not its content.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Keypress.Shift(Keypress.Enter).show   // t"[⇧]+[↵]"
      Keypress.Ctrl('C').show               // t"[⌃]+[C]"
      Keypress.FunctionKey(12).show         // t"[F12]"
      ```
